---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="File Formats"
  description="Every container and wire format in the game that we've decoded, in plain English. Start with RESO and LZ11 — they wrap everything else."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'File Formats' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    The game's files are layered. A single <code>.lzs</code> file is
    usually a compressed wrapper around a container, and the container
    holds several smaller files inside it. Each of those smaller files
    has its own format. So before you can look at, say, a tile sheet
    inside the title screen pack, you have to unwrap two or three
    layers.
  </p>

  <p>
    These pages cover each layer in order — the outer wrappers first,
    then the containers, then the actual asset formats inside them.
  </p>

  <h2>The wrapping order</h2>
  <p>
    Most asset files are wrapped like this:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`.lzs file
  └─ LZS wrapper (16 byte header)
      └─ LZ11 compressed stream
          └─ RESO container
              ├─ tile sheet (NCGR)
              ├─ palette (NCLR)
              ├─ sprite definition (NCER)
              └─ background tilemap (NSCR)`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    A <code>.ofs</code> file skips the LZS wrapper and the compression —
    it's just a RESO container directly.
  </p>

  <div class="fg-cards">
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/reso/`} class="fg-card">
      <h3>RESO containers</h3>
      <p>The main container format. A simple table of contents pointing at chunks of data. Two header variants and a list of type tags for the chunks.</p>
    </a>
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/lz11/`} class="fg-card">
      <h3>LZS / LZ11 compression</h3>
      <p>The compression wrapper Nintendo uses on most assets. About 70 percent of files in the game are LZ11-compressed.</p>
    </a>
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/nitro-graphics/`} class="fg-card">
      <h3>Nintendo Nitro graphics formats</h3>
      <p>NCGR, NCLR, NCER, NSCR, NANR. The five Nintendo formats that hold all the actual image data. Standard across most DS games.</p>
    </a>
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/konami-extensions/`} class="fg-card">
      <h3>Konami's custom extensions</h3>
      <p>Tags that aren't standard Nintendo. <code>tad</code> holds widget tables; <code>nib</code>, <code>rnan</code>, <code>sfo</code> wrap other kinds of binary data inside RESO containers.</p>
    </a>
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/resc/`} class="fg-card">
      <h3>RESC containers</h3>
      <p>A newer container with named entries. Used for the item-slot data files and a handful of other places.</p>
    </a>
  </div>

  <h2>The Tinke / Nintendo language tax</h2>
  <p>
    Almost every format here has its magic bytes stored <strong>backwards</strong>.
    The RESO file you'd expect to start with <code>RESO</code> does start
    with <code>RESO</code>, but the entry tags inside it are reversed —
    <code>rgcn</code> in the file means the entry is an NCGR, <code>rlcn</code>
    means NCLR, and so on. This is just little-endian byte order showing
    through: the engine reads a 4-character tag as a 32-bit integer, so the
    bytes appear reversed when you stare at the file in a hex editor.
  </p>
</FieldGuideLayout>
